== Free Threading

Standard CPython enforces a Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) @Python315GILDocs that serializes Python bytecode execution across threads. To bypass this, pygrametl relies on `multiprocessing` in CPython or with Jython @JythonProject, which runs on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) without the GIL. However, Jython remains pinned to Python 2.7, whereas pygrametl targets Python 3 except for some Jython support.

PEP 703 proposes to makes the GIL optional in CPython @PEP703 @PyFreeThreadingGuide, allowing multiple threads to execute Python bytecode concurrently. We decided to use the free-threaded CPython build (`3.15t`) to disable the GIL and evaluate whether free-threading can eliminate the overhead of `multiprocessing`. Replacing pygrametl's `multiprocessing` layer with a dedicated free-threading library @GabaFreeThreading proved infeasible, as the library `free-threading` has strict pickling requirements, which would require refactoring of pygrametl. Instead, we retained the existing `multiprocessing` architecture, and extended it with Python's `threading` module @Python315ThreadingDocs when the GIL is disabled. This preserves full API compatibility with existing pygrametl workflows, automatically dispatching threads when the GIL is disabled while falling back to `multiprocessing` on GIL-enabled builds.

For the evaluation, we built CPython `3.15t` with `--disable-gil` to remove the GIL and enable concurrent bytecode execution @Python315ConfigureDocs @PEP703. We also compiled the interpreter with `--enable-optimizations`, which profiles a representative workload across multiple build stages to optimize code paths, and `--with-lto`, which optimizes and inlines functions across separate C source files during linking @Python315ConfigureDocs.